Day 5/300: Windows Command Line Deep Dive – Moving Beyond the GUI

Today marks Day 5 of my 300-day cybersecurity commitment. I wrapped up the "Windows Command Line" module on TryHackMe, and I want to share what I actually learned—not just theory, but practical, actionable skills that solve real problems.

What I Mastered Today:

1. File System Navigation & Manipulation

Navigated complex directory structures using cd, dir, and tree to map out system hierarchies.

Performed bulk file operations with copy, xcopy, move, and del—automating tasks that would take minutes in GUI in seconds.

Used robocopy for robust file replication with error recovery, a lifesaver for backup scripts.

2. Process & Task Management

Leveraged tasklist with filters to identify rogue processes consuming memory.

Killed unresponsive applications remotely using taskkill /PID—essential for incident response when RDP is down.

Scheduled automated maintenance tasks using schtasks, understanding how attackers use this for persistence.

3. Networking & Troubleshooting

Used ipconfig /all to diagnose DHCP and DNS misconfigurations.

Mapped network latency and packet loss using ping -t for continuous monitoring.

Analyzed active connections with netstat -anob to spot suspicious outbound traffic—a core skill for threat hunting.

Cleared DNS cache with ipconfig /flushdns to resolve domain resolution issues instantly.

4. System Information & Security

Retrieved hardware and OS details using systeminfo—critical for vulnerability assessments.

Managed user accounts and group memberships with net user and net localgroup.

Checked patch levels and uptime to verify compliance with security policies.

5. PowerShell Cmdlets (The Game Changer)

Explored PowerShell's Get-Command, Get-Help, and Get-Process—realizing its superiority over CMD for automation.

Started scripting simple one-liners to filter logs and export results to CSV for reporting.

Why This Matters for Cybersecurity:

Incident Response: When a system is compromised, the GUI is often slow, broken, or infected. The CLI is your lifeline.

Forensics: Command-line tools allow you to collect volatile data (running processes, network connections) before it disappears.

Automation: Patching, log analysis, and user management must be automated at scale—CLI makes this possible.

Understanding Attackers: Many ransomware strains and post-exploitation tools rely on native Windows commands. Knowing these helps you detect and defend.

The Challenge I Solved Today:

I encountered a scenario where a system was unresponsive due to a memory leak. Using tasklist and taskkill, I identified and terminated the offending process, restored system performance, and used schtasks to schedule a daily reboot—all without a single click.

Daily Accountability:

I'm documenting every command, cheat sheet, and solve on my GitHub (link in comments). This isn't about vanity—it's about building muscle memory and proving that consistency compounds.

To Fellow Learners:

Don't dismiss the Windows CLI as "old school." It is the bedrock of enterprise security. Whether you're defending Active Directory, hunting threats, or responding to incidents, these commands are your toolkit.

300 days is a long journey. But today, I'm one day stronger.

